WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.300 --> 00:00:04.111
Module Seven, Lesson One: the NVIDIA agentic stack, mapped.

2
00:00:04.441 --> 00:00:05.565
Here's who does what.

3
00:00:06.365 --> 00:00:11.136
Picture Leo, a new architect at a furniture retailer, in his first design review.

4
00:00:11.466 --> 00:00:16.237
Colleagues say NIM, Triton, Curator and Customizer; he can't tell them apart.

5
00:00:17.037 --> 00:00:20.699
The fix is to learn each piece's one-line job, layer by layer.

6
00:00:21.029 --> 00:00:23.728
On top sit Blueprints, then the agent layer:

7
00:00:23.778 --> 00:00:27.102
the NeMo Agent Toolkit plus frameworks like LangGraph.

8
00:00:27.902 --> 00:00:32.460
Then NIM serves models, and NeMo microservices run the model lifecycle.

9
00:00:32.790 --> 00:00:36.324
At the base: the serving runtime and Kubernetes infrastructure.

10
00:00:37.124 --> 00:00:41.191
NIM itself is a prebuilt, GPU-optimised model container.

11
00:00:41.521 --> 00:00:44.927
The NIM Operator manages those services on Kubernetes.

12
00:00:45.727 --> 00:00:49.560
NeMo Retriever does extraction, embedding and reranking for RAG.

13
00:00:49.890 --> 00:00:54.085
NeMo Guardrails enforces programmable safety rails at runtime.

14
00:00:54.885 --> 00:00:59.955
Curator prepares data, Customizer fine-tunes, and Evaluator runs benchmarks.

15
00:01:00.285 --> 00:01:03.563
The Data and Entity Stores hold artefacts and metadata.

16
00:01:04.363 --> 00:01:07.975
The NeMo Agent Toolkit composes agents across frameworks,

17
00:01:08.025 --> 00:01:09.839
adding profiling and tracing.

18
00:01:10.169 --> 00:01:12.798
Triton is a multi-backend inference server;

19
00:01:12.848 --> 00:01:15.601
Dynamo spreads inference across a datacentre.

20
00:01:16.401 --> 00:01:18.037
Four boundaries trip people up.

21
00:01:18.367 --> 00:01:20.941
NIM is a packaged, model-specific service,

22
00:01:20.991 --> 00:01:24.439
while Triton is a general server you configure yourself.

23
00:01:25.239 --> 00:01:28.539
Curator prepares the data, and Customizer trains on it.

24
00:01:28.869 --> 00:01:33.939
Evaluator measures quality offline, while Guardrails enforce policy on live traffic.

25
00:01:34.739 --> 00:01:38.955
And the Agent Toolkit instruments agents built in frameworks like LangGraph.

26
00:01:39.285 --> 00:01:41.646
It composes them; it doesn't replace them.

27
00:01:42.446 --> 00:01:43.719
So why is NIM fast?

28
00:01:44.049 --> 00:01:49.147
Its TensorRT-LLM engine brings in-flight batching, a paged KV cache,

29
00:01:49.197 --> 00:01:53.236
quantisation, prefix caching and speculative decoding.

30
00:01:54.036 --> 00:01:56.229
With an OpenAI-compatible API,

31
00:01:56.279 --> 00:02:01.389
switching from a hosted endpoint to NIM is usually a base-URL change.

32
00:02:01.719 --> 00:02:03.845
So agent code doesn't need rewriting.

33
00:02:04.645 --> 00:02:08.468
Exam trap: asked why NIM beats a naive Hugging Face server,

34
00:02:08.518 --> 00:02:11.357
name those mechanisms, not “it's optimised”.

35
00:02:11.687 --> 00:02:13.109
Read on for the full table.
